[h1]Heroes Evolve[/h1]

Lords are handed an equipment set when they come of age and then nothing ever
happens to it. Some are handed a broken one -- a single weapon, or civilian
clothes to fight a war in. The rest keep what they were given at eighteen until
the day they die, while their skills sit still beside it.

This mod gives them a career instead: it repairs the broken ones, lets all of
them improve at what they do, and sends them to market to spend their own clan's
money. Nothing is granted free that a lord could buy, and nothing is bought that
his skills do not justify.

[h2]What it does[/h2]

Four systems, each a switch, described as what they do to your campaign.

[b]1. Repairs lords the game left broken.[/b] Every day it checks each lord for
two specific faults: fewer than two weapons, or body armour of tier 1, which is
civilian clothing. Either one gets him a complete kit -- weapons, armour, a
mount if he already rode -- picked to match his own skills, at the lowest tier
that qualifies. It never gives him the best available, and it runs once: after
that he is on his own and has to buy the rest.

[b]2. Grows skills for the rest of a lord's life.[/b] Weekly, it moves each lord
toward a ceiling of his own, closing 12% of the remaining gap a year, capped at
4.2 points in any one skill so nothing runs away. Weapon skills follow the
weapons he carries; the rest follow where he has put focus. The ceiling is his
talent times 150 at sixty, and talent comes from his own id, so it is identical
in every save and no two lords share it. Reaches lords, hired companions and
wanderers still in taverns -- so a companion hired at forty is worth what those
years bought, and costs more, since price rises with level. Notables are left
alone.

[b]3. Sends lords shopping with their own money.[/b] Entering a town, a lord has
a 25% chance of visiting the market, at most once a day. If he goes, he may
spend up to 30% of his clan's purse, divided between the slots that town can
improve for him -- so a lord who needs everything comes back in middling gear,
and one who needs a single piece buys it well. What he may wear at all is capped
by his combat skill: one tier per 28 points, so tier 6 needs about 168. The gold
is real and leaves the clan, and a reserve is held back so buying gear can never
stop a clan paying its troops.

[b]He only ever improves what he already carries.[/b] Shopping never fills an
empty slot, never changes what kind of fighter he is, and never mounts a man
fighting on foot -- though it will buy a better horse for one already riding. A
swordsman looks for a better sword; he does not come back with a bow. Whatever
he holds is what gets better, whether this mod's repair handed it to him or you
did.

[b]4. Lets a captor strip his prisoner.[/b] A captor may take everything the man
wears: weapons, armour, horse and harness, typically ten of the eleven slots at
once. All or nothing, no half-robbery. It moves into the captor's inventory,
where he wears it or sells it, and is never destroyed -- so beating him later
puts it on your loot screen.

Whether he does it is his character, not a die roll: honour weighs heaviest,
then generosity and mercy equally, against roguery and how the two men stand. A
father will not strip his son unless they are already at war. Bandits always do.
At the shipped setting the median captor robs one prisoner in sixteen, the most
honourable one in a hundred, the worst one in four -- roughly one lord in nine
stripped somewhere on the map in a year. It costs him ten points of standing
with the man and a mark against his honour.

The victim is not left in his shirt: he is repaired the day he gets out and buys
his way back up. Quest items, crafted weapons and unique gear are never taken.

[h2]How you rob someone[/h2]

Nothing else in this mod asks anything of you. This is the one part you operate,
and it is a conversation.

[list=1]
[*]Take a lord prisoner, or hold one in a town or castle your clan owns.
[*]Talk to him. In your own party, that is the prisoner list on the party
screen; in a settlement, it is the dungeon.
[*]Pick [i]"Hand over your arms and armour."[/i] It sits in the same menu as
"I have decided to let you go."
[/list]

The line changes with the man. A lord whose own honour is negative is told there
is none in him for you to offend, and robbing him costs half -- the game already
halves the penalty for executing a dishonourable prisoner, and this follows it.
A friend gets "do not take this personally", at full price. Everyone else gets
the plain demand. All three answer you, and the answers are worth reading.

You are never rolled for: nothing makes you rob a man and nothing stops you. It
costs twelve points of standing with him and a mark against your honour, halved
against the dishonourable, and you gain Roguery for what you took.

The option appears only when there is something to take and the man is yours. If
you cannot see it, he has nothing left worth taking, or robbery is off.

[h2]The same rules for everyone[/h2]

You are not exempt and you are not privileged. Lords rob your prisoners on the
same terms you rob theirs, except that nothing ever decides for you.

That includes your own house. Your family, and the companions you have given a
party or a caravan to, are lords the AI takes into battle, so they are repaired,
they grow, and they go shopping like anyone else. If you would rather outfit
them yourself, one switch moves that line around your whole clan.

Gear is where the line falls, never learning. Heroes riding in your own party
keep the equipment you gave them -- that inventory is yours -- but they go on
improving like everyone else, so a companion at your side for twenty years does
not quietly fall behind the map. Nothing overshoots: a hero already at his
ceiling receives nothing.

[h2]Configuration[/h2]

Sixteen settings in the in-game options screen if you have Mod Configuration
Menu, and in [i]settings.xml[/i] if you do not. MCM is optional, not required.
Every setting takes effect immediately -- no restart, no reload.

Turn off any of the four systems, exempt your own clan, move the gear ceiling,
change how freely lords spend, scale or disable the robbery, and strike item ids
from what lords may buy. A one-click census surveys every lord in your campaign
to a log file -- the same survey every number here was measured with, so you can
check them against your own game rather than taking them on trust.

[h2]Compatibility[/h2]

[list]
[*][b]No Harmony patches.[/b] Nothing in the game engine is rewritten.
[*][b]Writes nothing into your save.[/b] The mod keeps no data of its own.
[*][b]Safe to add or remove mid-campaign.[/b] Add it and lords improve from
wherever they are. Remove it and the campaign carries on, though lords keep what
they bought, as they would keep anything else they own.
[*]Works with mods that add items: new gear is part of what lords may buy, under
the same rules. Incendiary weapons are refused outright, and you can exclude
anything else by id.
[*][b]War Sails is not required, and is what this was built on.[/b] Every figure
above was measured on campaigns running the naval DLC. It depends on none of it
and runs the same without.
[/list]

[h2]Requirements[/h2]

[list]
[*]Mount & Blade II: Bannerlord [b]v1.4.8[/b]
[*][i]Optional:[/i] Mod Configuration Menu (MCM) v5, for the in-game settings screen
[/list]

[h2]Languages[/h2]

English, Spanish, French, German, Russian.

[h2]Installation[/h2]

Extract into your [i]Modules[/i] folder and enable "Heroes Evolve" in the
launcher. Load it after MCM if you use it.

[h2]Notes[/h2]

Nearly every number here was measured on live campaigns rather than guessed, and
several were corrected when the measurement disagreed with the design.

Bug reports welcome. Turn logging on, reproduce it, and send [i]hev.log[/i] from
the Bannerlord logs folder -- it usually says exactly what happened.
